# === dsh-departments patch: dsh-tool-fs-search GREP SCOPE DECLARATION (fb-765) ===
#
# WHAT THIS FIXES
#   A whole-tree `grep` answered 0 matches for a symbol that EXISTS in a path
#   ripgrep ignores (`.gitignore`), with nothing in the result distinguishing
#   "it is not there" from "it was never looked for". Measured on the installed
#   tree (2026-09-11): the compiled-only symbol `__publicField` matches 0 files
#   under ripgrep's default rules and 1 file with `--no-ignore` (a gitignored
#   `lib/` artifact); a `grep` for a symbol in the gitignored
#   packages/dshd-orchestration/lib/delivery.js returned only its non-ignored
#   hits. A silent false negative becomes a LIE in a report.
#
# WHAT THIS PATCH DOES — AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
#   `buildGrepCommand`'s ARGV IS NOT TOUCHED (D-765-R1): honoring `.gitignore`
#   is the established norm for content search (5/5 reference tools honor it;
#   0/5 widen it), the narrower walk is the cheaper one, and `--no-ignore
#   --hidden` would descend into `.git/objects` because ripgrep prunes VCS
#   metadata only through the dotfile rule. The discarded "argv parity with
#   buildGlobCommand" direction would have raised scan cost AND exposed VCS
#   metadata; the sibling `glob` argv is untouched here too.
#   What changes is the OUTPUT: every `grep` result — INCLUDING a zero-match
#   one — now carries a SCOPE DECLARATION stating what was not searched, why,
#   and how many files.
#
# HOW THE DECLARATION IS MEASURED (bounded, attributed, never fatal)
#   After the search, three `rg --files` LISTING passes (no content read, no
#   match records) differing only in the two scope switches the search has off:
#     default = ignore rules on, dotfiles hidden  -> the set the search walked
#     hidden  = + --hidden                        -> + what the dotfile rule removed
#     all     = + --no-ignore                     -> + what the ignore rules removed
#   The include filter and the search root mirror the real call, so the counts
#   are THIS call's scope. GLOB_VCS_EXCLUDES is pinned in all three passes:
#   ripgrep prunes `.git/` only by the dotfile rule, so without the pin the
#   `all` pass counts VCS internals as content files (measured: 8884 -> 14432
#   listed paths on one repository) and the declaration would overstate scope.
#   Cost is a fraction of the search itself (measured 25-70 ms per listing pass
#   against ~26 ms for the search on a 14k-file tree) and every pass is bounded
#   by the SAME deployment caps as the search (rawOutputMaxBytes, graceMs,
#   stderrMaxBytes, cooperative timeout). A pass that cannot complete NEVER
#   fails the search: the reason returns by error CODE and the declaration says
#   the count could not be measured — a silent 0 would be this same defect with
#   another name (verified: a shrunk rawOutputMaxBytes yields a declaration
#   naming SEARCH_RAW_OUTPUT_OVERFLOW).
#
# ADDITIVE / COMPATIBILITY
#   The match body, the `Found X of Y` header and the spill-recovery footer are
#   byte-identical to before; the declaration is a final paragraph appended to
#   the model-facing text. A canonical value WITHOUT a `scope` field (an older
#   replayed session) renders exactly as before. `presentationMeta` (the search
#   card) is deliberately NOT extended: `meta` is serialized with the session
#   log and re-sent on every request, so the declaration rides in the text. The
#   new `scope` field is declared in the tool's output schema (not required).
#
# TARGET FILES
#   <npm-root>/@deepseek-ai/dsh/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs-search/lib/index.js
#
# FINGERPRINTS (md5)  [path-not-found-applied rc.2 (the live base) -> patched]
